In the design of electron transport layers (ETLs) to enhance the efficiency of planar perovskite solar cells (PSCs), facile electron extraction and transport are important features. Here, we consider the effects of different titanium oxide $\mathrm{(TiO}_{2})$ polymorphs, anatase and brookite. We design and fabricate high-phase-purity, single-crystalline, highly conductive, and lowtemperature $(<180^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ -processed brookite based $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ heterophase junctions on fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) as the substrate. We test and compare single-phase anatase (A) and brookite (B) and heterophase anatase–brookite (AB) and brookite–anatase (BA) as ETLs in PSCs. The power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) of PSCs with low-temperature-processed single-layer FTO-B as the ETL were as high as $14.92\%$ , which is the highest reported efficiency of FTOB-based single-layer PSC. This implies that FTO-B serves as an active phase and can be a potential candidate as an n-type ETL scaffold in planar PSCs. Moreover, the surface of highly crystalline brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ exhibits a tendency toward inter-particle necking, leading to the formation of compact scaffolds. Furthermore, PSCs with heterophase junction FTO-AB ETLs exhibited PCEs as high as $16.82\%$ , which is superior to those of PSCs with single-phase anatase (FTO-A) and brookite (FTO-B) as the ETLs ( $13.86\%$ and $14.92\%$ , respectively). In addition, the PSCs with FTO-AB exhibited improved efficiency and decreased hysteresis compared with those with FTO-BA $(13.45\%)$ due to the suitable band alignment with the perovskite layer, which resulted in superior photogenerated charge carrier extraction and reduced charge accumulation at the interface between the heterophase junction and perovskite. Thus, the present work presents an effective strategy to develop heterophase junction ETLs and manipulate the interfacial energy band to further improve the performance of planar PSCs and enable clean, and eco-friendly fabrication of low cost mass production.
Keywords: Low-temperature processing; Brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles; $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ heterophase junction; planar perovskite solar cells.
In the past several years, organic–inorganic metal-halide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have emerged as one of the most promising designs in third-generation thin-film photovoltaics. They offer a unique combination of desirable properties including high absorption, tunable bandgap, and long carrier diffusion length and can be fabricated easily and inexpensively by solution processing. Their power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) have been rapidly enhanced from $3.8\%$ in early versions to $23.3\%$ .1-5 The electron transport layer (ETL) plays an indispensable role in transporting photogenerated electrons and blocking holes to eliminate the electrical shunt and yield highly efficient PSCs.6, 7 Titanium oxide (TiO2) is widely considered to be one of the most promising materials for the ETLs in PSCs because its conduction band (CB) closely matches that of perovskite, thus facilitating electron injection and collection. However, the application of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL to PSCs remains limited by drawback such as low conductivity and large amounts defects, which inevitably lead to carrier recombination.8 Notable efforts have been made to resolve these drawbacks to enhance the performances of PSCs. The use of doping9, nanocomposites10, 11, and interface engineering12-15 are among the most effective strategies.  The hysteresis phenomenon can be attributed to various factors including the rich number of oxygen vacancies, charge trapping, ion motion, and ferroelectricity16, 17. The passivation of the compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer with $\mathrm{C_{60}/P C B M^{18}}$ or doped TiO219, 20 can be used to tune the energy level to match the CB of perovskite and, thus, enhance the electron collection and transport and reduce the hysteresis.
The three most widely used distinct crystalline polymorphs of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ —anatase (tetragonal), brookite (orthorhombic), and rutile (tetragonal)—have different structures and, thus, unique properties. The surface morphology and properties of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer significantly influence the quality of the perovskite layer in terms of the crystal size, uniformity, and surface coverage, all of which affect the photovoltaic performance. Therefore, it is fundamentally important to investigate the photovoltaic performance of different polymorphs of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ in order to understand the role of microstructure in $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -based PSCs. Anatase is broadly used as an ETL material in PSCs due to its low cost, environmentally friendly nature, high transparency, tailorable electronic properties, and compatibility with perovskite in terms of the energy level. Park et al.21 demonstrated that rutile $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , which has a more positive CB edge potential than anatase, resulted in a lower open-circuit voltage ( $\mathrm{(}V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ . On the other hand, brookite has the highest band gap energy of these three $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ polymorphs and, thus, has the most negative CB level. Due to these properties, brookite exhibits a higher $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ than other phases as a photoanode material in dye-sensitized solar cells.22 Therefore, it is important to investigate brookite-based $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ heterophase junction as ETL for boosting the charge transport property of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ semiconductor heterophase junctions and as well as their efficiency of PSCs.
Heterophase junctions with built-in potentials have been commonly used in optoelectronic conversion devices, involving solar cells. Zhu et al.23 developed PSCs with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ anatase/rutile phase junctions. Furthermore, the CB edge of brookite is slightly above those of anatase and rutile, which is expected to promote electron transfer from brookite to anatase; this provides a potential new route to manipulate the charge transport at $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ heterophase junctions in semiconductor devices.
However, conventionally, highly acidic solutions and organic solvents are used in the synthesis of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . Thus, common titanium compounds cannot be used in these synthesis methods due to their rapid hydrolysis, toxicity, flammability, and tendency to form corrosive by-products. In contrast, a pure-phase, non-toxic, and environmentally friendly hydrothermal technique24 has been developed using a water-soluble titanium complex as the titanium source. From an environmental point of view, these water-soluble complexes of titanium have proven to be very promising reagents for the preparation of titanium-containing functional materials as they have lower carbon contents per titanium atom. This method has been used to produce brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ with a nanoparticulate morphology. However, it remains a challenge to use the brookite nanoparticles (NPs) for an efficient ETL phase junction bilayer in planar PSCs in terms of achieving good charge transport, suppressing hysteresis, and realizing good photovoltaic performance.
In this study, we use the environmentally friendly hydrothermal synthesis technique using a water-soluble titanium complex as the titanium source to prepare pure-phase single-crystalline brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs.24 We leverage these NPs to, for the first time, combine opposite-phase anatase–brookite (AB) and brookite–anatase (BA) structures on fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) substrates to manipulate the charge transfer in TiO2 semiconductor heterophase junctions. We then utilize these structures as ETLs in planar PSCs. These phase junctions are expected to reduce the agglomeration and trapping and improve the number of photogenerated charge carrier sites at the interface between the ETL and the perovskite, which should enhance the efficiency and reduce the hysteresis. Herein, we compare the effectiveness of single-phase anatase (A) and brookite (B) and heterophase anatase–brookite (AB) and brookite–anatase (BA) as ETLs in PSCs. Specifically, we observe the morphological, optical, and structural characteristics of the ETLs; evaluate the interface between the ETLs and perovskite; and measure the performances of PSCs with different ETLs.
## Experimental methods
## Materials
FTO-coated glass substrates with a sheet resistance of $^{1}0\Omega\mathrm{sq}^{-1}$ were obtained from Asahi Glass (Tokyo, Japan). The perovskite precursors were methylammonium bromide (MABr), formamidinium iodide (FAI), cesium iodide (CsI), lead bromide (PbBr2), and lead iodide (PbI2); all were obtained from TCI (Tokyo, Japan). N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.5\%$ purity) and dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO, $99.5\%$ purity) were purchased from Wako Chemical (Tokyo, Japan). Titanium diisopropoxide bis(acetylacetonate) ( $75\mathrm{\wt\%}$ in isopropanol, SigmaAldrich) was dissolved in a 2-isopropanol ( $99.9\%$ purity, Wako Chemical) to a concentration of $0.25{\mathrm{M}}{}$ . Pure-phase brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs with nanorod shapes were prepared at $\mathsf{p H}10$ by hydrothermal treatment according to a previous report by Tomita et al.24 All of these commercially available materials were used as received without further purification.
## Synthesis of brookite TiO2 NPs
Fine titanium powder (5 mmol) was dissolved in a solution of $30\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (aq.) and $28\%$ NH3 (aq.). Glycolic acid $7.5\mathrm{mmol}\AA$ ) was added, and the solution was then dried at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to remove the excess $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ . Next, a Ti-glycolate complex solution was prepared by dissolving the product in distilled water to a concentration of $0.25{\mathrm{M}}{}$ . Sodium acetate (45 mmol) was dissolved in the Ti-complex solution, and the mixture was heat-treated in an autoclave at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{h}$ . The formed $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ particles were separated by centrifugation and washed twice with distilled water. The $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs were finally dispersed in distilled water to prepare a colloidal suspension.
## Device fabrication
FTO-patterned glass substrates were cleaned sequentially in a sonication bath in commercial detergent in water followed by deionized water followed by acetone followed by ethanol; each cleaning step was $15\mathrm{min}$ . The cleaned FTO-coated substrates were then dried with nitrogen and treated with UV-ozone for 15 min. Compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was deposited on the FTO substrate at an optimized thickness of $60\mathrm{nm}$ as follows: As reported by Wakamiya et al.25, FTO-A was deposited by spray pyrolysis deposition of the precursor solution ( $_{0.25\mathrm{M}}$ titanium diisopropoxide bis(acetylacetonate) in isopropanol), whereas the substrate was on a hotplate at $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ; the substrate was then baked for $30\mathrm{min}$ in a muffle furnace then allowed to cool to room temperature. The FTO-B ( $100\mathrm{nm}$ thick) was fabricated by spin-coating the synthesized noncolloidal suspension of pure-phase brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs onto the FTO substrates in five coating cycles at $5000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30~\mathrm{s}$ each cycle; the coating was then annealed at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 5 min and further annealed on a hot plate at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . To prepare the FTO-AB structure, the brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs were spin-coated on the FTO-A ( $\mathrm{\Delta}60\mathrm{\nm}$ thick) in three coating cycles (optimum condition) of $5000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30~\mathrm{s}$ . To prepare the FTO-BA structure, a $60\mathrm{nm}$ thick brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NP layer was formed on the FTO substrate by three spin-coating cycles of 5000 rpm for 30 s and a $60\mathrm{nm}$ layer for anatase was then deposited on the brookite layer as described above. A triple-cation precursor solution with $5\%$ Cs was prepared and spin-coated over the ETL layer as reported previously9 and described briefly as follows: Stock solutions of FAI (1 M), PbI2 (1.1 M), MABr $(0.2\mathbf{M})$ , and PbBr2 $(0.2\mathbf{M})$ were mixed in anhydrous DMF:DMSO (4:1), and an appropriate volume of a $1.5{\mathrm{M}}{}$ CsI stock solution in DMSO was added to reach a final concentration of $5\%$ Cs. The perovskite solution was then spin-coated in three steps: 0 rpm for $15\mathrm{~s~}$ followed by $1000\mathrm{rpm}$ for 10 s followed by $6000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $20~\mathrm{s}$ . In the second step, ${500\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of chlorobenzene was dripped 5 s before the spin-coating step was completed. The deposited solution was then annealed at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $60\mathrm{min}$ . The chemical formulation of the deposited triple-cation perovskite was thus $\mathrm{Cs_{x}(F A_{0.83}M A_{0.17})_{(1-x)}P b(I_{0.83}B r_{0.17})_{3}}$ . The optimized thickness was about $350\mathrm{nm}$ . For the hole-transport layer (HTL), a spiro-OMeTAD precursor solution was prepared from dissolving spiro-OMeTAD $(200.77\mathrm{mg}$ ; Merck, Germany), lithium salt $(23.4\mathrm{mg})$ , cobalt $(7.3~\mathrm{mg})$ , and 4-tert-butylpyridine ( $70.2\upmu\mathrm{L}$ ; Sigma
Aldrich) in $2340~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of chlorobenzene by vigorous stirring at 900 rpm at room temperature for $2\mathrm{h}$ . This precursor was spin-coated on the perovskite layer at 3000 rpm for 30 s. The optimized thickness was about $200\mathrm{nm}$ . Finally, the electrode was formed by thermal evaporation of Au at a thickness of $80\mathrm{nm}$ on the HTL layer to complete the device.
## Characterization
Raman's spectroscopy (STR150, AIRIX, Tokyo, Japan) using a 50 mW, 532 nm diode laser was carried out to further confirm the selective synthesis of brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs. The NP size was measured using scanning transmission electron microscopy (STEM; HF-2200 TU, Hitachi Ltd., Tokyo, Japan). The morphological, optical, and structural characteristics of the ETLs and perovskite thin-films were observed using field emission scanning electron microscopy (SEM; S-4800, Hitachi High-Tech, Tokyo, Japan), a UV–Vis spectrophotometry (UV-Vis-NIR; V670, Jasco Corporation, Tokyo, Japan), and X-ray diffractometry (D8 Discover, Bruker AXS Co. Ltd., Tokyo, Japan) with an X-ray tube ( $\mathrm{{CuKa}}$ radiation, $\lambda=1.5406\mathrm{\AA}$ ), respectively. The charge transport properties of different ETLs on perovskite were measured by photoluminescence (PL; FP-8600, Jasco Corporation, Tokyo, Japan) and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS; E4980A Precision LCR Meter, Agilent, Tokyo, Japan). The current density vs. voltage characteristics of all fabricated PSCs were measured at a scan rate of $0.05\mathrm{V/s}$ in forward scan (FS; from $-0.1$ to $1.2\mathrm{V}$ ) and reverse scan (RS; from 1.2 to $-0.1\mathrm{V}$ ) configurations. Each device had an active area of $0.09\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . Measurements were obtained under $100\mathrm{mW/cm^{2}A M}1.5\mathrm{G}$ irradiation simulated by a solar simulator as measured using a Keithley 2401 digital source meter. The incident photon-to-electron conversion efficiency (IPCE) spectrum of each device was measured using a monochromatic xenon arc light system (Bunkoukeiki, SMI-250JA).
## Results and discussion
## Formation of brookite TiO2 NPs
Highly crystalline brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ leads to a higher driving force with a more negative flatband potential, and more effective charge transfer kinetics with low-resistance.26 Brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ phase has also been reported to have high conductivity and high thermal stability than anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ phase.27 Figure 1a shows the XRD pattern of brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs. Diffraction peaks were observed at 2θ angles of $25.4^{\circ}$ , $30.8^{\circ}$ , $36.3^{\circ}$ , $40.2^{\circ}$ , $46.1^{\circ}$ , and $48.0^{\circ}$ , which correspond to the orthorhombic phase of brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (ICSD No. 15409). The purity of the brookite phase in the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs can be confirmed by the main peaks at $25.3^{\circ}$ and $25.7^{\circ}$ which correspond to the (120) and (111) crystal planes, respectively; they are broadened and overlapping because of the nonparticulate character of the sample. In addition, the main peak at $30.8^{\circ}$ is assigned to the (121) crystal plane, which further confirms the purity. The phase purity was further confirmed by the Raman spectrum28 shown in Figure S1a (Supporting Information). The mean crystal size of the brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was $10.85\mathrm{nm}$ as calculated by Scherrer equation29 from the intensity of the peak at $30.8^{\circ}$ . In addition, the nanoscale morphology and particle sizes of the brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs were characterized by STEM. As shown in Figure 1b, the brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs were rod like in shape, and the particles ranged from 30 to $50\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter. Figure S2 (Supporting Information) shows the transmittance spectra of FTO-A, FTO-B, FTO-AB, and FTO-BA. The FTO-B exhibited better optical transparency than the FTO-A, with a transmittance above $80\%$ in the entire visible range.
Figure 2a, b shows energy band diagrams of the fabricated planar PSCs devices with heterophase junctions. Here, the relative energy band position of brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was drawn based on a previous report.30 It should be noted that the CB of brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is slightly higher than that of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . The device with the FTO-AB ETL exhibits a more favorable energylevel alignment with the perovskite layer due to its higher capacity for extracting photogenerated charges; it also generates a higher $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ , which will be discussed later. On the other hand, the brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ creates an inconsistency in CB with anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and, consequently, hinders the electron recombination in the device with an FTO-BA $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL.
with (a) FTO-AB and (b) FTO-BA as the ETLs.
The XRD patterns of the perovskite films on different $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layers and that of a perovskite film without $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ are depicted in Figure S3 (Supporting Information). Diffraction peaks were detected at 2θ angles of $14.01^{\circ}$ , $28.40^{\circ}$ , $31.91^{\circ}$ , and $40.74^{\circ}$ in perovskite films with FTO-A, FTO-B, FTO-AB, and FTO-BA; the peaks were assigned to the (110), (220), (310), and (224) crystal planes, respectively.31 In the perovskite with the FTO-AB $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ phase junction, the XRD peak at $14.12^{\circ}$ was slightly stronger and sharper than in the other spectra, indicating higher crystallinity in this ETL; this was attributed to the superior crystallinity of the synthesized single-crystalline brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ as shown in Figure 1a.
Top-view SEM images of FTO-A, FTO-B, FTO-AB, and FTO-BA are shown in Figure 3a– d. The compact anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ( $60\mathrm{nm}$ thickness) deposited by spray pyrolysis exhibited a few pinholes on its surface (Figure 3a). Single-phase brookite TiO2 NPs deposited by spin-coating on an FTO substrate were denser scaffolds (Figure 3b). These scaffolds may offer efficient charge extraction, superior electron pathways, and probably an exceptional hole-blocking effect because of the dense packing. When brookite TiO2 NPs were homogenously distributed on FTO-A, they formed a uniform and dense scaffold (FTO-AB) without visible pinholes (Figure 3c). Thus, this ETL should facilitate the efficient separation of charges and reduce the recombination rate. In contrast, when a compact anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer was deposited on FTO-B by spray pyrolysis, a dense scaffold was formed (FTO-BA), indicating that the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layers were evenly coated on the FTO (Figure 3d).
The morphological characteristics of the perovskite layers on FTO-A, FTO-B, FTO-AB, and FTO-BA are shown in Figure S4a–d (Supporting Information). In the perovskite on FTO-AB, large crystals with uniform morphologies could be seen (Figure S4c, Supporting Information); because there are fewer grain boundaries and fewer traps, the charge carrier losses that generally occur at the grain boundaries and in the trap states should be reduced.17 Recently, we have demonstrated that anatase TiO2 NP layers encourage the growth of large grain perovskite films, resulting in significant performance enhancements in PSCs.11
In addition, EIS was conducted to investigate the electrical properties of each interface including the charge transfer, carrier recombination, and inner series resistance $(R_{\mathrm{s}})$ . Figure S6a (Supporting Information) shows the Nyquist plots of each device at a DC bias of $0\mathrm{v}$ in the absence of light. According to the equivalent circuit model shown in Figure S6 (inset; Supporting Information), the detailed fitting parameters for $R_{\mathrm{s}}$ and recombination resistance $(R_{\mathrm{rec}})$ are summarized in Table S1 (Supporting Information). The device with the FTO-AB heterophase junction structure as the ETL exhibits a smaller $R_{\mathrm{s}}$ and much larger $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ , indicating more efficient extraction and transport of electrons across the FTO-AB/perovskite interface, which is attributed to the type-II band alignment. This should result in a larger short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ , $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ , and fill factor (FF) than in devices with single-phase FTO-A and FTO-B or heterophase FTO-BA.
To investigate the roles of single-phase FTO-A and FTO-B and heterophase FTO-AB and FTO-BA as ETL layers, we fabricated 10 PSC devices with each layer. The photocurrent density–voltage $\left(J\ –\nabla\right)$ curves and IPCE spectra of the fabricated devices are shown in Figure 5. The corresponding photovoltaic parameters are summarized in Table 1 including the $J_{\mathrm{sc}},V_{\mathrm{oc}},$ FF, and PCE, and $R_{\mathrm{s}}$ ; a comparison of the FS and RS results are summarized in Table S2. In order to investigate the reproducibility of the PSC performance, 10 devices with each type of ETL (FTO-A, FTO-B, FTO-AB, and FTO-BA) were fabricated. The average $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ , $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ , FF, and PCE values for each type of PSC are compared in Figure S8. The standard deviation (expressed as error bars in Figure S8) shows good reproducibility and stable performance in all devices, but the results were more reproducible in the devices with FTO-AB ETLs. Table 1 shows that the champion PSC with an FTO-A ETL (i.e., that with the highest PCE) exhibited a considerably lower $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ , $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ , FF, and PCE than the other champion devices. However, the champion PSC with an FTO-B ETL exhibited the highest performance of single-phase FTOB-based PSC with a PCE of $14.92\%$ . This implies that a low-temperature-processed singlephase FTO-B can be used as an efficient ETL in regular planar PSCs. This is attributed to the large interface area (Figure S1b; Supporting Information) for electron injection and the following transport that further balances the charge carriers. However, the devices with singlephase ETLs exhibited significant hysteresis due to the contact barrier that prevented efficient electron injection at the interface between FTO-A and perovskite (Figure 5b) or FTO-B and perovskite (Figure S7; Supporting Information), which facilitates strong leakage current and recombination of charge carriers. Prior studies26, 27 have attributed the higher conductivity of brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ than anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to the faster transport of photogenerated electrons to the electrodes, reducing the charge carrier recombination and generating of more reactive radicals. Furthermore, the PSCs with FTO-AB ETLs exhibit significantly better performance than those with single-phase FTO-A and FTO-B as ETLs with PCEs as high as $16.82\%$ . The enhancement of $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and FF upon addition of the brookite layer to FTO-A was attributed to the lowering of the injection barrier at the interface between FTO-A and perovskite because the FTO-AB heterophase junction forms a type-II structure and, thus, facilitates the carrier flow. This result indicates that the insertion of a brookite layer on the FTO-A substrate enhanced the charge carrier extraction owing to the higher conductivity from the perovskite layer to the FTO electrode, thus improving the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and PCE of the resulting films. Moreover, PSCs with FTOAB ETLs exhibited a significantly lower average $R_{\mathrm{s}}$ of $62.41\pm8.84\Omega\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ compared with those with FTO-A, FTO-B, and FTO-BA ETLs; this suggests that FTO-AB heterophase junction enhanced the carrier extraction (Figure 4a and Figure S6) from the perovskite to the FTO electrode, thus improving the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ , $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ , FF, and PCE. Furthermore, in FTO-AB heterophase junction, the brookite CB edge is slightly higher than anatase and more closely matches the band structure of perovskite, thus boosting the $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ compared with other phases of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ counterparts. The enhancement of the FF is also attributed to the reduction of the leakage current and lower $R_{\mathrm{s}}$ . In addition, the FTO-AB TiO2 heterophase junction based PSCs further exhibited less hysteresis, which is likely due to the larger grain size of the perovskite (as shown in Figure S4c) with fewer trap states and the higher electron extraction efficiency, which minimizes the number of recombination carriers.32 It is assumed that the more negative CB band of brookite may induce electron transfer from brookite to anatase, thus enhancing the photo-induced charge transfer and electron-hole separation and reducing the hysteresis. In addition, the interface between anatase and single-crystalline brookite may encourage interfacial electron transport and hinder the recombination of electron-hole pairs, making this ETL more effective than single-phase ETLs of anatase or brookite. On the other hand, the PSC with the FTO-BA ETL exhibited a PCE of $13.45\%$ , which was higher than that with the FTOA ETL was lower than those with heterophase ETLs. Since the CB of brookite is slightly higher than that of anatase, the electron transport from perovskite to the FTO-B in the FTO-BA serves as a barrier layer that hinders the electron recombination. Thus, the performance of the PSCs with FTO-BA ETLs indicated that they had inferior photovoltaic properties. We also investigate the effects of brookite the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer thickness on the performance of the FTO-AB TiO2 heterophase junction-based PSCs by changing the number of coating cycles. Figure S9 (a, b; Supporting Information) shows the photovoltaic properties of the PSCs prepared with two, three, four, and five coating cycles of brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs (40, 60, 80, and $100\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively) on FTO-A ( $\mathrm{\Delta}60\mathrm{\nm}$ thick). The photovoltaic parameters are summarized in Table
S3.
Finally, the IPCE was measured to further demonstrate the accuracy of the output $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ . The PSCs with FTO-A, FTO-B, FTO-AB, and FTO-BA ETLs produce integrated photocurrents of 18.30, 20.26, 20.51, and $17.05\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , respectively, as shown in Figure 5d. The spectral responses of those with FTO-AB and FTO-B extended from the visible region to the near-infrared spectral region (approximately 380–680 nm wavelength coverage) with a broad, flat absorption peak of around $87-90\%$ intensity. In contrast, the IPCE value was about $70\text{\textperthousand}$ in the PSCs with FTO-A and FTO-BA over the same wavelength coverage. The widerranging IPCE spectrum of the device with FTO-AB compared with that with FTO-BA shows that the FTO-AB $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ heterophase junction collects electrons more efficiently at the interface between the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and perovskite, which efficiently reduces the interfacial energy barrier.
