## Conclusions
Here, we demonstrated the fabrication of pure-phase, single-crystalline, and low-temperature processed brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs on FTO as ETL for planar PSCs. We found that planar PSCs make the FTO-B as the ETL scaffold exhibited the highest efficiency to date $(14.92\%)$ . The large surface area of brookite TiO2 NPs may have facilitated efficient electron injection and subsequent transport, which can balance the electron current density and hole current density in the resultant PSC and, thereby, promote efficient performance. We expect that further enhancements can be made to single-phase FTO-B-based planar PSCs in terms of device performance by engineering the brookite $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer to ensure a good interface with the perovskite, reduce the hysteresis, and incorporate extrinsic doping to eliminating the defects.
Furthermore, we have demonstrated, for the first time, the use of TiO2 opposite-phase (FTOAB) and (FTO-BA) to manipulate the charge transfer in $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ semiconductor junctions as ETLs in planar PSCs. We showed that PSCs with FTO-AB $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ as the ETL exhibited improved efficiency up to $16.82\%$ . They also exhibited negligible hysteresis compared with the devices having single-phase FTO-A, FTO-B, and FTO-BA ETLs. Notably, the FTO-AB $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ heterophase junction ETL exhibits a more favorable energy level alignment with the perovskite layer, resulting from its higher capacity for photogenerated charge carrier extraction and reduced recombination at the interface between the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and perovskite as evidenced by the PL and EIS analyses. On the other hand, the devices based on the FTO-BA heterophase junction ETL exhibit inconsistent CBs of brookite and anatase, which hinders the electron recombination and results in a lower PCE of $13.45\%$ . Thus, the control of the interfacial energy band by manipulating the ETL structure has been proven to be an encouraging new opportunity for fabricating highly efficient planar PSCs.
